    content_en:
      'Almost everyone has experienced the surprisingly sharp sting of a paper cut. It\'s a tiny wound, yet it can hurt far more than a knife cut. Why does something so small cause so much pain?\n\nThe biggest reason is the density of nerve endings in your fingertips. Your fingertips are among the most nerve-rich areas of your entire body, with thousands of pain receptors per square centimeter. Even a minuscule injury sends intense pain signals to your brain.\n\nAdditionally, paper edges are much rougher than they appear. Under a microscope, paper looks like a jagged saw blade. Instead of making a clean cut, paper tears and shreds the skin cells, causing more tissue damage than a sharp knife would.\n\nTo make matters worse, paper cuts are usually too shallow to bleed. While that might sound like a good thing, bleeding actually helps by forming a clot that seals the wound from air. Without that protective covering, the exposed nerve endings remain irritated by air and movement. Next time you\'re handling paper, you might want to be a little more careful!',
